Unsloth (and PyTorch in general) cannot use an AMD GPU and an NVIDIA GPU together in tandem for a single training job. You cannot split a model or shard tensors across them because they rely on completely incompatible communication backends (NVIDIA uses NCCL, AMD uses RCCL). You must pick one ecosystem or the other.
However, your RTX 5090 (32GB)
should actually be capable of fine-tuning a 27B model if you use the correct Unsloth settings. You are likely running out of memory (OOM) because of the "Max Context" requirement, not the model size itself.
Here is how to fix the VRAM issue so you can train on the 5090
alone, and why the mixed setup won't work.
1. Why "Mixed" GPUs Don't Work
Training across multiple GPUs (Distributed Data Parallelism or Model Sharding) requires the cards to synchronize gradients milliseconds-by-millisecond. 

    The Language Barrier: NVIDIA cards speak CUDA and sync via NCCL. AMD cards speak ROCm and sync via RCCL. They cannot talk to each other to coordinate weights during a training step.
    The OS Limitation: Windows (WSL2) rarely allows efficient peer-to-peer access between different GPU drivers in a way that supports tensor splitting.

2. How to Fit 27B on One RTX 5090
A 27B model in 4-bit (QLoRA) occupies about 16-17 GB of VRAM for weights. This leaves ~15 GB for context (gradients and activations). To fit a large context (like 32k-128k) into that remaining space, you must enable these specific Unsloth features:

