and during inference can i change the model context back to it's maxiumum? what about a Gemma4 E4B model?
Yes, during inference you can completely restore the context window to its native maximum limit (e.g., 128K or 256K), provided you used an architecture that dynamically scales its RoPE (Rotary Position Embeddings) parameters during the training phase. 
When fine-tuning on a shorter context (like truncating to 8K or 16K to avoid OOM errors on your hardware), the model does not permanently "forget" how to handle long context. Instead, it scales dynamically via RoPE during inference, provided you configure your engine properly. 
1. Re-extending Context in Inference
When loading your saved LoRA adapters or merged weights into an inference engine (like vLLM, Ollama, or Hugging Face Transformers), the engine initializes the context length based on your inference configurations rather than your training truncation limit. 

    How to change it: Pass the max length argument back to your runner:
        vLLM: Use --max-model-len 131072
        Ollama (Modelfile): Set PARAMETER num_ctx 131072 
    The Catch: While the model can mechanically handle the full length, its accuracy at long ranges (Needle-in-a-Haystack metrics) might degrade if it never saw long text samples during your fine-tuning phase. To prevent this, include at least a small percentage of long-context examples in your dataset during training.

2. The Gemma 4 E4B Model Variant
The Gemma 4 E4B (Effective 4 Billion) model behaves exceptionally well for this specific workload because of its highly optimized on-device architecture. 

    Native Max Context: It has a default maximum context window of 128K tokens. 
    Hybrid Attention: The "E" family handles long context memory differently than standard heavy models. It features an interleaved attention loop: it uses a lightweight 512-token local sliding window for fast generation speed, but hooks into full global layers with p-RoPE (Proportional RoPE) for deep, long-context awareness across the full 128K field. 
    The VRAM Benefit: Because of this architectural optimization, an E4B model (which scales down to roughly 4.5B active parameters during calculations) will comfortably run at its full 128K context during inference on your RTX 5090, taking up only a fraction of your 32GB VRAM buffer. 
    Multimodal Edge: E4B
    supports interleaved text, images, and native audio (speech-to-text / translation) completely encoder-free via Unsloth GGUF or standard deployment pipelines. 

