For training and tuning Gemma 4 E4B (4.5B effective parameters) and the companion small architectures (like E2B or the 12B Unified model), you can absolutely set the context length to its maximum limit (128K tokens) right from the start using Unsloth on your single RTX 5090. 
Because the "E" (Effective) family uses unique on-device architectural tricks, training them requires significantly less VRAM than traditional dense architectures. Your 32GB VRAM card is more than enough to handle their max context windows. 
1. Why E4B Fits at Max Context (128K)
Traditional models crash when you scale training context because they compute a massive global matrix across every single token. Gemma 4 E4B completely sidesteps this via a hybrid attention mechanism: 

    Sliding Window Integration: It restricts intensive calculations to a 512-token local sliding window.
    Interleaved Global Layers: It uses Proportional RoPE (p-RoPE) and unified Key-Value caching only on a few designated global layers.
    Per-Layer Embeddings (PLE): It handles token mapping via efficient lookups rather than raw structural weight overhead. 

This means its VRAM footprint scales linearly instead of exponentially during long-context training, making a 128K context fine-tune fully viable on your single RTX 5090. 
2. The Unsloth Training Configuration
To train the E4B variant at its maximum 128K ceiling, configure your Unsloth load script exactly like this: 
python

from unsloth import FastVisionModel 
import torch

# 1. Load the optimized E4B variant 
model, tokenizer = FastVisionModel.from_pretrained(
    model_name = "unsloth/gemma-4-E4B-it", # Or your preferred base variant
    load_in_4bit = True,                  # Drastically cuts activation memory
    max_seq_length = 131072,              # 128K Max Context Ceiling
)

# 2. Apply PeFT / LoRA target modules
model = FastVisionModel.get_peft_model(
    model,
    r = 16,
