    lora_dropout = 0,
    bias = "none",
    use_gradient_checkpointing = "unsloth", # Crucial for saving activation memory
)

Use code with caution.
3. Essential Training Arguments for 128K
Even with a lightweight 4B effective architecture, filling up a 128K sequence with multi-modal inputs (like interleaved text, images, or native audio features supported by E4B) will cause VRAM spikes if your batch mechanics are set incorrectly. Use these precise parameters in your trainer: 

    per_device_train_batch_size = 1: Do not go higher than 1. Use gradient_accumulation_steps = 4 or 8 to achieve your actual desired batch size without storing active step calculations in VRAM.
    optim = "adamw_8bit": Keeps optimizer state footprint compressed.
    group_by_length = True: Highly critical for long-context multi-modal datasets. This ensures short prompts are packed together and 128K long prompts are handled independently, preventing massive padding overhead from wasting your VRAM. 

4. What about an "E8" model?
Google's Gemma 4 naming scheme uses E2B (2.3B effective / 5.1B total) and E4B (4.5B effective / 8B total with embeddings). 

    If you are referring to the 8B total parameter footprint of the E4B, it follows the exact same 128K limits detailed above.
    If you are looking at stepping up to their intermediate multi-modal tier, the Gemma 4 12B Unified model or their larger 26B A4B (Mixture of Experts) variants expand natively to an even larger 256K maximum context window. On a 32GB RTX 5090, you can push the 12B model to its absolute limits, or comfortably quantize and tune the 26B variant using the exact same Unsloth setup. 

