and which model would give me the best performance with less regression at 1m context with multimodality?
For the best performance at 1M context with minimal regression ("lost-in-the-middle" issues) while retaining multimodal capabilities, the current top recommendation for June 2026 is Qwen 3.6 Plus (or the Qwen 3.7-Max if you have API access), followed closely by the new MiniMax M3.
While Gemma 4
is excellent for on-device efficiency, it natively caps at 256K. Stretching it to 1M via RoPE scaling often introduces severe reasoning degradation compared to models trained explicitly for that depth.
Top Pick: Qwen 3.6 Plus (The "Solid" 1M All-Rounder)
This is currently the most robust open-weight choice for preserving reasoning over massive contexts.

    Context Stability: It features a "solid 1M context" that is specifically cited for maintaining full recall on hours-long video inputs and book-length documents. 
    Regression Resistance: Unlike models that just "support" 1M but get confused, Qwen 3.6 uses a "preserve_thinking" mode (similar to Chain-of-Thought) that prevents attention drift deep in the context window. 
    Multimodality: It handles Text, Images, and Video natively.
        Note on Audio: While it excels at Video (visual + transcript alignment), if you strictly need raw native audio processing (like listening to a WAV file directly without a transcript), you would need the Qwen-Omni variant, which might have a shorter context than the main text/vision dense model. 

Runner Up: MiniMax M3 (The Coding Specialist)
Released in early June 2026, this is a "frontier-class" open-weight model that targets the same 1M niche. 

    Performance: It currently tops the open-weight SWE-Bench (coding) leaderboards, making it potentially better than Qwen for massive repository-level code refactoring. 
    Architecture: It uses "MiniMax Sparse Attention" (MSA) which reduces decoding costs by ~15x, meaning it runs faster at 1M context than Qwen. 
    Trade-off: It is generally less battle-tested for general-purpose creative writing or "roleplay" compared to the Qwen ecosystem.

Why not Gemma 4 for 1M?
Although Gemma 4 (especially the 26B and 31B variants) is incredible, its architecture is optimized for 256K.

    The Limit: Unsloth and other loaders can technically stretch it to 1M, but benchmarks show high regression (hallucination) past the 300K-400K mark because its "local sliding window" attention mechanism struggles to retain global coherence over that distance.
    Use Case: Stick to Gemma 4 only if your data fits within 256K and you specifically need its native, encoder-free Audio capabilities. 

