You cannot compile weights directly into a raw standalone .apk or .ipa (iOS app) from a shell compiler script, because weights are just raw data (numbers), not executable application code.
To actually get an application you can click on your phone, you must package those weights inside a compiled application wrapper (a UI shell built with Kotlin, Swift, or React Native) that knows how to read them. 
Here is how you actually get an installer on your phone depending on your goals:
Option 1: Use Pre-Built Shells (No App Development Required)
If you don't want to build a whole app from scratch and just want to test your fine-tuned model on your phone right now, you can load your compiled model files into an open-source "shell" app. 

    For Android: You can download the official MLCChat.apk directly from the mlc-ai/binary-mlc-llm-libs GitHub Releases page. Once installed on your phone, you can transfer your compiled model folder to your phone's internal storage via a USB cable or adb push to load it. 
    For iOS: Apple strictly forbids raw sideloading or direct .ipa installation without a developer profile. You can download MLC Chat directly from the Apple App Store. Once installed, you can configure it to point to a Hugging Face repository where you have uploaded your custom model weights. 

Option 2: Build and Bundle a Native .apk / Xcode Project
If you are developing a proprietary application and want to bundle your custom vision and tool-calling models natively so the app works completely offline without network downloading, you use Android Studio or Xcode to compile the actual application wrapper around your files. 
How the Android .apk Build Works:
Your compiled .tar model weights are placed inside the project assets folder. The Gradle build process then packages those weights, the native C++ hardware abstraction layers, and your Kotlin UI logic into a single installer. 
